// =============================================================================
//  HR ANALYTICS - DAX MEASURES AND CALCULATED COLUMNS
//  Report: HR_Attrition_Dashboard.pbix
// =============================================================================
//
//  ABOUT THIS FILE
//  This file lists all the DAX formulas used in the Power BI dashboard:
//  2 calculated columns and 18 measures. Next to each formula there is a short
//  note with the question it answers and how it works.
//
//  The same results were also calculated with SQL, Python, and Excel.
//  All four tools give the same numbers.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  DATA MODEL
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Tables (loaded from data/clean):
//      Employee         1,470 rows    one row for each employee
//      Performance      6,709 rows    one row for each performance review
//      EducationLevel   5 rows        names of the education levels
//
//  Names and ethnicity were removed from the Employee table in Power Query,
//  because they are not needed for the analysis.
//
//  Relationships (one-to-many, single direction):
//      Employee[EmployeeID]              (one)  ->  Performance[EmployeeID]  (many)
//      EducationLevel[EducationLevelID]  (one)  ->  Employee[Education]      (many)
//
//  Because of the first relationship, a slicer on an Employee column
//  (for example Department) also filters the reviews.
//
//  Two simple terms:
//      Calculated column = a new column. Its value is calculated once for each row.
//      Measure           = a calculation that changes when the user clicks a
//                          slicer or when it is used in a different chart.


// =============================================================================
//  1. CALCULATED COLUMNS (in the Employee table)
// =============================================================================

// Puts each employee in an age group, so we can compare attrition by age.
// SWITCH checks the conditions from top to bottom and returns the result of
// the first one that is true. It is easier to read than many nested IFs.
Age Group =
SWITCH (
    TRUE (),
    Employee[Age] <= 25, "18-25",
    Employee[Age] <= 35, "26-35",
    Employee[Age] <= 45, "36-45",
    "46-55"
)

// Splits employees into new (0-2 years) and longer-serving (3+ years).
// We use it for the high-risk group, because many employees leave in the
// first two years.
Tenure Band =
IF ( Employee[YearsAtCompany] <= 2, "0-2 years", "3+ years" )


// =============================================================================
//  2. MAIN KPIs
// =============================================================================

// How many employees are in the current selection?
// COUNTROWS counts the rows that are left after the slicers and filters.
Total Employees = COUNTROWS ( Employee )

// How many of them left the company?
// CALCULATE counts the rows again, but only where Attrition = "Yes".
Employees Left =
CALCULATE ( COUNTROWS ( Employee ), Employee[Attrition] = "Yes" )

// What share of employees left?
// DIVIDE is safer than "/". It shows a blank value instead of an error
// when the number below is zero.
Attrition Rate = DIVIDE ( [Employees Left], [Total Employees] )


// =============================================================================
//  3. SALARY
// =============================================================================

// A few very high salaries make the average high. The median (the middle
// value) shows a typical salary better.
Median Salary = MEDIAN ( Employee[Salary] )

// Shown next to the median, so we can see the difference between them.
Average Salary = AVERAGE ( Employee[Salary] )


// =============================================================================
//  4. OVERTIME
// =============================================================================

// What is the attrition rate for employees who work overtime, and for those
// who do not?
// A filter on a column inside CALCULATE replaces the slicer on that same column.
// So these two measures ignore the OverTime slicer. This is on purpose: one
// chart can show both values together.
Overtime Attrition Rate =
CALCULATE ( [Attrition Rate], Employee[OverTime] = "Yes" )

No Overtime Attrition Rate =
CALCULATE ( [Attrition Rate], Employee[OverTime] = "No" )

// How much higher is attrition with overtime?
// This is the difference between two percentages, so the unit is percentage
// points. The value 0.201 is shown as 20.1%, and it means 20.1 points.
Overtime Gap = [Overtime Attrition Rate] - [No Overtime Attrition Rate]


// =============================================================================
//  5. FIRST TWO YEARS AND THE HIGH-RISK GROUP
// =============================================================================

// What is the attrition rate in the first two years at the company?
First 2 Years Attrition Rate =
CALCULATE ( [Attrition Rate], Employee[YearsAtCompany] <= 2 )

// High-risk group = employees who work overtime AND have 0-2 years at the company.
// When CALCULATE has more than one filter, all of them must be true.

// How many employees are in this group?
High-Risk Employees =
CALCULATE (
    [Total Employees],
    Employee[OverTime] = "Yes",
    Employee[YearsAtCompany] <= 2
)

// How many of them left?
High-Risk Leavers =
CALCULATE (
    [Employees Left],
    Employee[OverTime] = "Yes",
    Employee[YearsAtCompany] <= 2
)

// What is the attrition rate in this group?
High-Risk Attrition Rate = DIVIDE ( [High-Risk Leavers], [High-Risk Employees] )

// What share of all employees who left come from this group?
// If a slicer is selected (for example Department = Sales), both numbers are
// calculated inside that selection.
High-Risk Share of Leavers = DIVIDE ( [High-Risk Leavers], [Employees Left] )


// =============================================================================
//  6. PERFORMANCE REVIEWS (in the Performance table)
// =============================================================================

// Number of reviews. It is not the number of employees: one employee can have
// several reviews, and 190 employees have no review.
Reviews = COUNTROWS ( Performance )

// Average ratings, calculated over all reviews. An employee with more reviews
// has more weight. SQL and Excel use the same method.
Avg Self Rating = AVERAGE ( Performance[SelfRating] )

Avg Manager Rating = AVERAGE ( Performance[ManagerRating] )

// A negative value means managers give lower ratings than employees give
// themselves.
Rating Gap = [Avg Manager Rating] - [Avg Self Rating]


// =============================================================================
//  7. DATA CHECK
// =============================================================================

// How many employees have no review?
// FILTER looks at each employee. RELATEDTABLE returns that employee's reviews.
// If the number of reviews is 0, the employee is counted.
Employees Without Review =
COUNTROWS (
    FILTER ( Employee, COUNTROWS ( RELATEDTABLE ( Performance ) ) = 0 )
)


// =============================================================================
//  EXPECTED VALUES (WITH NO SLICER SELECTED)
// =============================================================================
//  Total Employees                 1,470
//  Employees Left                  237
//  Attrition Rate                  16.1%
//  Median Salary                   71,200 (71,199.5 before rounding)
//  Average Salary                  112,956
//  Overtime Attrition Rate         30.5%
//  No Overtime Attrition Rate      10.4%
//  Overtime Gap                    20.1 percentage points
//  First 2 Years Attrition Rate    29.7%
//  High-Risk Employees             154
//  High-Risk Leavers               74
//  High-Risk Attrition Rate        48.1%
//  High-Risk Share of Leavers      31.2%
//  Reviews                         6,709
//  Avg Self Rating                 3.98
//  Avg Manager Rating              3.47
//  Rating Gap                      -0.51
//  Employees Without Review        190
//
//  Example with the slicer Department = Sales:
//  Total Employees 446, Employees Left 92, Attrition Rate 20.6%.


// =============================================================================
//  LIMITS
// =============================================================================
//  - The measures show relationships, not causes. For example, attrition is
//    higher with overtime, but this does not prove that overtime is the reason.
//  - Some groups are small (for example the HR job roles), so their rates can
//    change a lot with only a few employees. The job role chart shows only
//    roles with 30 or more employees.
//  - The data has no commission or sales target columns, so these measures
//    cannot explain the high attrition of Sales Representatives.
//  - 1,141 reviews have a date before the hire date of the employee. The rows
//    are kept, and no measure here uses review dates.
